# Security response headers for Cloudflare Pages. Static -- Pages serves
# this file's rules on every response with no build step, which is why it
# lives in public/ rather than being generated.
#
# script-src and connect-src name Cloudflare Web Analytics (the beacon
# Cloudflare Pages injects, and where it reports to) -- without them the
# browser blocks it and no visit is ever recorded.
#
# img-src also names media.api-sports.io: team crests are loaded straight
# from the data provider's public image CDN (see serializers.team_to_schema).
#
# connect-src and img-src name *.onrender.com and api.soccaintel.com --
# the backend moved from Render's own subdomain to the Oracle VM behind
# api.soccaintel.com (see DEPLOYMENT.md's Option A), and this file is
# static (no build step reads VITE_API_URL into it), so the move had to be
# mirrored here by hand. *.onrender.com is kept rather than removed: Render
# stays up as a rollback target during the migration, and this header is
# what silently breaks every API call -- with no network request even
# reaching the backend to show an error -- the moment it's out of sync with
# wherever the frontend is actually configured to call.
#
# style-src needs 'unsafe-inline': the app uses React's inline style={{...}}
# extensively (every page in src/pages, src/components), which CSP treats as
# untrusted unless explicitly allowed. That's a real trade-off -- an
# attacker who achieves HTML injection could still inject a <style> tag or
# style attribute -- but script-src (the much higher-value target for an
# attacker: arbitrary JS reads localStorage, including the session token)
# stays locked to 'self' with no such exception, and nothing in this
# codebase uses dangerouslySetInnerHTML or innerHTML (verified during the
# audit that added this file), so there's no injection point for either to
# land through in the first place.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security: Cloudflare Pages already 301s plain http:// to
# https:// (verified directly against production), but that redirect is a
# single request an on-path attacker, a captive portal, or a stale cached
# response can intercept or strip before the browser ever sees it. HSTS
# closes that window for every return visit by telling the browser to never
# even attempt http:// for this host again, no redirect required.
# preload is left off: submitting to the HSTS preload list is a one-way
# door (removal takes months to propagate through browsers) and should be a
# deliberate decision, not a side effect of this file.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: https://*.onrender.com https://api.soccaintel.com https://media.api-sports.io; connect-src 'self' https://*.onrender.com https://api.soccaintel.com https://cloudflareinsights.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
